te/Assistant Professor of Speech-Language Pathology |focus on adult neurogenic comm disorders Associate/Assistant Professor of Speech-Language Pathology |focus on adult neurogenic comm disorders 515057 Denton, Texas, United States Faculty Title: Associate/Assistant Professor of Speech-Language Pathology |focus on adult neurogenic comm disorders Employee Classification: Assoc Professor Campus: University of North Texas Division: UNT-Provost SubDivision-Department: UNT-Col of Publ Affairs & Hlth Sci Department: UNT-Audiology & Speech - Lang Path-135370 Job Location: Denton Salary: Compensation is competitive and commensurate with the candidate\u2019s qualifications, experience, and discipline, in accordance with university guidelines and available funding. FTE: 1.00 Retirement Eligibility: ORP Eligible About Us - Values Overview Welcome to the University of North Texas System. The UNT System includes the University of North Texas in Denton and Frisco, the University of North Texas at Dallas and UNT Dallas College of Law, and University of North Texas Health Fort Worth. We are the only university system based exclusively in the robust Dallas-Fort Worth region. We are growing with the North Texas region, employing more than 14,000 employees, educating a record 49,000+ students across our system, and awarding nearly 12,000 degrees each year. We are one team comprised of individuals who are committed to excellence, curiosity and innovation. We are transforming lives and creating economic opportunity through education. We champion a people-first values-based culture where We Care about each other and those we serve. We believe that we are Better Together because we foster an environment of respect, belonging, and access for all. We demonstrate Courageous Integrity through setting exceptional standards and acting in the best interest of our communities. We are encouraged to Be Curious about opportunities for learning, creating, discovering, and innovating, and are encouraged to learn from failure. Show Your Fire by joining our team and exhibiting your passion and pride in your work as part of our UNT System team. Learn more about the UNT System and how we live our values at www.UNTSystem.edu . Department Summary The Department of Audiology & Speech-Language Pathology (ASLP), located in College of Public Affairs and Health Sciences (PAHS) at the University of North Texas, provides the finest graduate professional education in both speech-language pathology and audiology and maintains an excellent undergraduate degree program. The department stresses excellence in teaching, research, and service by its faculty and students. As part of the educational programs, the department operates the UNT Speech and Hearing Center, which offers professional services to members of our campus community and the general public for the purpose of providing clinical training for students. The Speech and Hearing Center offers state-of-the-art diagnosis and treatment of speech, language, and hearing disorders. The College of Public Affairs and Health Sciences (PAHS) at the University of North Texas prepares graduates to address diverse and complex social issues such as aging, disability, disaster management and recovery, criminal justice, health disparities and public safety, and find solutions to challenges in public administration, public and mental health, exercise physiology, and urban design. We have nine academic departments with undergraduate and graduate programs, the Kristin Farmer Autism Center, the Center for Public Management, the UNT Speech and Hearing Center, the UNT Work Inclusion and Supported Employment (UNTWISE) program, UNT ELEVAR, and several professional development and clinical training programs to facilitate careers to address a global society. UNT has a highly diverse campus with a wide range of languages spoken in addition to English. We welcome candidates who have experience with HSI/MSIs and/or who speak Spanish, Vietnamese, American Sign Language, Chinese (Cantonese, Mandarin and other variations), Arabic, Tagalog, Farsi, French, or/and Yoruba. Position Overview The Department of Audiology and Speech-Language Pathology in the College of Public Affairs and Health Sciences at the University of North Texas invites applications for a 9-month tenure-track assistant or associate professor with expertise in speech-language pathology to begin Fall 2027. Successful candidates will have a Ph.D. in speech-language pathology, communication sciences and disorders, or a related field. Preferred candidates will bring an externally-funded research program, with expertise in neurodiverse populations or adult neurogenic communication disorders, including aphasia, cognitive-communication disorders, traumatic brain injury, right hemisphere disorders, dementia, and other neurological conditions. They will also help to educate and mentor future speech-language pathologists through excellence in teaching, clinical education, and research. This is an exceptional opportunity to join one of the nation's leading public research universities, where interdisciplinary collaboration, clinical innovation, and translational research improve the lives of individuals, families, and communities. The successful candidate will: \u2022 Maintain, develop, and sustain externally funded research \u2022 Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in speech-language pathology and related disciplines. \u2022 Mentor master's and doctoral student research while fostering a collaborative and innovative research environment. \u2022 Supervise graduate research, including theses and dissertations. \u2022 Contribute to clinical education through supervision of student clinicians in the UNT Speech and Hearing Center and affiliated healthcare partners. \u2022 Publish impactful research in leading peer-reviewed journals and present findings at national and international conferences. \u2022 Contribute to curriculum development, accreditation activities, departmental governance, and professional service. We welcome applicants in any area of speech-language pathology, with preference given to those with research programs focused on neurogenic communication disorders or neurodiverse populations. Areas of interest include: \u2022 Neurogenic communication disorders, including aphasia, cognitive-communication disorders, and communication changes associated with stroke, traumatic brain injury, and neurodegenerative disease \u2022 Neurodiversity and developmental communication differences, including autism \u2022 Neuroplasticity, rehabilitation, and functional communication outcomes \u2022 Augmentative and alternative communication (AAC) and other communication supports \u2022 Technology-based interventions, telepractice, implementation science, and health disparities research Minimum Qualifications \u2022 Ph.D. in speech-language pathology, communication sciences and disorders, or a closely related discipline. \u2022 An established research agenda with the ability to engage in independent research and grant development activities \u2022 Ability to teach in traditional and non-traditional formats and settings (on-campus, off-campus, evenings, online) and utilize instructional technology software (instructional software, learning management systems such as Canvas) \u2022 Demonstrated excellence in graduate teaching (in traditional and non-traditional formats), clinical education, and student mentorship. Preferred Qualifications \u2022 An established record of peer-reviewed publications demonstrating scholarly excellence. \u2022 Evidence of a sustained, externally funded research program. \u2022 Demonstrated excellence in graduate teaching (in traditional and non-traditional formats) and student mentorship. \u2022 National or international recognition in adult neurogenic communication disorders. \u2022 Experience supervising graduate student clinicians and clinical fellows. \u2022 Leadership experience within academic programs or professional organizations. Required License/Registration/Certifications Physical Requirements Communicating with others to exchange information. Environmental Hazards No adverse environmental conditions expected. Work Schedule varies based on assignment Driving University Vehicle No Security Sensitive This is a Security Sensitive Position. Special Instructions Applicants must submit a minimum of two professional references as part of their application. If needed, additional references can be added after the application has been submitted. Join Us! We're seeking a collaborative colleague who is passionate about advancing the field of speech-language pathology, mentoring future speech-language pathologists, and translating research into innovative clinical practices. If you're ready to expand an impactful research program while helping shape the future of speech-language pathology, we invite you to apply. Applicant Documents \u2022 Cover Letter \u2022 Curriculum Vitae \u2022 Description of 5-year research plan \u2022 Three letters of reference upon committee request \u2022 Statement of teaching interests \u2022 If applicable, evidence of teaching effectiveness Benefits For information regarding our Benefits, click here . EEO Statement The University of North Texas System is firmly committed to equal opportunity and does not permit \u2013and takes actions to prevent \u2013 discrimination, harassment (including sexual violence, domestic violence, dating violence and stalking), and retaliation on the basis of race, color, religion, national origin, sex, age, disability, genetic information, or veteran status in its application, employment practices, and facilities; nor permits race, color, national origin, religion, age, disability, veteran status, or sex discrimination and harassment in its admissions processes, and educational programs and activities. UNT System Administration promptly investigates complaints of discrimination, harassment, and related retaliation and takes remedial action when appropriate. System Administration also takes actions to prevent retaliation against individuals who oppose any form of harassment or discriminatory practice, file a charge or report, or testify, assist, or participate in a related investigation or proceeding. Apply for This Job First Name (required) 5549dad7 Last Name (required) d34349f7 Email (required) 9684b8c9 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form and including your phone number, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at the University of North Texas System. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). Privacy Policy Thank you Sign Up for Job Alerts Job Alert bfe81f5d Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments UNT-Provost UNT-Provost Locations Denton, Texas, United States Denton, Texas, United States First Name (required) 3c5072ed Last Name (required) 588dab3b Email (required) 304ad15e Sign Up! Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form and including your phone number, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at the University of North Texas System. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). Privacy Policy Thank you Want to Share This Job? First Name (required) f100e9b1 Last Name (required) ffb5dab5 Email (required) 39ad94f8 Share to: 014507df Share Now! Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form and including your phone number, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at